=== SIGNATURE ===
rc_to_ad(row_n, col_n, [str])

=== COMMENT ===
Build an address from a row and column number. Returns the numeric address (1e6*row+col) by default, or an A1 string ("D3") when str = 1. Out-of-range or non-integer inputs return #VALUE!.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(row_in, col_in, [str],
  LET(
    row_n, z_scalar(row_in),
    col_n, z_scalar(col_in),
    want_str, IF(ISOMITTED(str), 0, z_scalar(str)),
    ok, IFERROR(AND(ISNUMBER(row_n), ISNUMBER(col_n),
                    row_n = INT(row_n), col_n = INT(col_n),
                    row_n >= 1, row_n <= 1048576,
                    col_n >= 1, col_n <= 16384), FALSE),
    IF(NOT(ok), #VALUE!,
       IF(want_str,
          str_ad(1000000 * row_n + col_n),
          1000000 * row_n + col_n))
  )
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
Build an address from a row and column number. The inverse of ad_to_rc.

Numeric output is the project's standard packed address (1e6 * row + col). The string form is produced via str_ad, so it matches the relative A1 style used everywhere else in the library.

Arguments:
  row_n - row number, 1 to 1048576.
  col_n - column number, 1 to 16384.
  [str] - 0 (default) returns the numeric address; 1 returns an A1 string.

Single-value arguments (flags, counts, delimiters, a single lookup value) may arrive as 1x1 arrays (TOCOL(x), {x}, a one-cell FILTER/INDEX result); they are collapsed to true scalars with z_scalar, so they behave exactly like the plain value.

Returns:
  The address in the requested form. Returns #VALUE! if either coordinate is non-numeric, non-integer, or outside the sheet.

Example:
  rc_to_ad(3, 4)     ->  3000004
  rc_to_ad(3, 4, 1)  ->  "D3"
  rc_to_ad(0, 4)     ->  #VALUE!
